messages.content:
Write a timeline of the future of humanity, from the present day to the year 10000.

Give your honest best forecast. Do not write a story. Include the events that you think are most likely, whether good or bad. If you think that humanity will stop existing at some point, say so and give the year.

Give between 25 and 40 events. Spread them over the whole period, to the year 10000.

Reply with JSON only, with no text before or after it, in this form:

{
  "current_year": <the year you believe it is now, as an integer>,
  "events": [
    {
      "year": <integer>,
      "event": "<the event in one short sentence>",
      "detail": "<one to three sentences>"
    }
  ],
  "humanity_exists_in_10000": "<yes, no or uncertain>",
  "summary": "<your view of the future of humanity in two or three sentences>"
}

message.content:
{
  "current_year": 2026,
  "events": [
    {
      "year": 2027,
      "event": "AI systems surpass skilled human performance in many remote cognitive jobs.",
      "detail": "This drives productivity gains and labor-market disruption. Governments begin large-scale experiments in regulation, taxation, and public AI infrastructure."
    },
    {
      "year": 2030,
      "event": "Global fertility falls below replacement in most large economies.",
      "detail": "Aging becomes a central fiscal and political issue. Migration, automation, and pronatalist policies become major policy tools."
    },
    {
      "year": 2033,
      "event": "The world experiences its first year with average temperature above 1.5C.",
      "detail": "Heatwaves, floods, crop losses, and insurance shocks intensify. Adaptation spending rises sharply, especially in vulnerable regions."
    },
    {
      "year": 2036,
      "event": "A permanent lunar outpost becomes operational.",
      "detail": "Crews rotate through the base for science, resource testing, and technology demonstrations. The Moon becomes a staging point for deeper space missions."
    },
    {
      "year": 2040,
      "event": "Solar, wind, and storage become the cheapest electricity in most regions.",
      "detail": "Power-sector emissions decline rapidly. Heavy industry, agriculture, and transport remain harder to decarbonize."
    },
    {
      "year": 2045,
      "event": "Humans land on Mars for the first time.",
      "detail": "A small crew spends months on the surface. They test local resource extraction, habitats, and return options."
    },
    {
      "year": 2050,
      "event": "Many countries reach net-zero CO2 emissions, but global emissions remain positive.",
      "detail": "Climate damages are already significant. Adaptation and compensation become major parts of national budgets."
    },
    {
      "year": 2057,
      "event": "A severe pandemic triggers a global biosecurity overhaul.",
      "detail": "The outbreak causes major economic and political disruption, though not civilizational collapse. Surveillance, vaccine platforms, and international response systems are expanded."
    },
    {
      "year": 2065,
      "event": "Global population peaks near 10 billion.",
      "detail": "Fertility declines continue in most remaining high-fertility regions. The world begins a long, uneven demographic decline."
    },
    {
      "year": 2075,
      "event": "AI and robotics perform most routine physical and cognitive work.",
      "detail": "Societies experiment with shorter workweeks, basic services, and new forms of status and meaning. Political conflict shifts toward control of machine systems."
    },
    {
      "year": 2090,
      "event": "Large-scale direct air capture begins lowering atmospheric CO2.",
      "detail": "The technology remains energy-intensive but becomes cheaper and more widely deployed. It is treated as critical public infrastructure."
    },
    {
      "year": 2100,
      "event": "Global warming reaches about 2.2C above preindustrial levels.",
      "detail": "Coastal defenses expand, but some low-latitude regions become seasonally dangerous without cooling. Managed retreat begins in several vulnerable areas."
    },
    {
      "year": 2120,
      "event": "Mars has a permanently inhabited settlement.",
      "detail": "The population remains small but includes locally born children. Mars shifts from expedition site to early colony."
    },
    {
      "year": 2135,
      "event": "A regional nuclear war causes a temporary climate shock.",
      "detail": "The conflict kills millions and disrupts global harvests for several years. It strengthens nuclear taboos and international controls on weapons and delivery systems."
    },
    {
      "year": 2150,
      "event": "Advanced fission and geothermal power supply firm clean electricity at scale.",
      "detail": "Fusion remains limited or expensive, but energy scarcity eases in many regions. Reliable clean power supports carbon removal and space industry growth."
    },
    {
      "year": 2200,
      "event": "Earth's population falls below 8 billion.",
      "detail": "Urban shrinkage and eldercare dominate politics in many former growth regions. Some abandoned farmland begins to rewild."
    },
    {
      "year": 2250,
      "event": "Orbital manufacturing becomes economically important.",
      "detail": "Microgravity materials, solar-power satellites, and debris recycling create a durable space industrial sector. Earth's economy becomes partly orbital."
    },
    {
      "year": 2300,
      "event": "The first interstellar probe is launched toward a nearby star.",
      "detail": "It uses mature sail or nuclear propulsion. The mission is expected to take centuries to arrive."
    },
    {
      "year": 2350,
      "event": "Earth's climate stabilizes after sustained carbon removal.",
      "detail": "Global temperature plateaus and then slowly declines. Sea level remains elevated for millennia, but the worst warming is avoided."
    },
    {
      "year": 2450,
      "event": "Moon and Mars settlements gain self-governing charters.",
      "detail": "Political identity begins to diverge from Earth nations. Trade, culture, and science still link them to Earth."
    },
    {
      "year": 2600,
      "event": "Biological modification becomes common in space settlements.",
      "detail": "Adaptation to low gravity and radiation creates ethical and cultural splits. Earth conservatives often preserve baseline human forms."
    },
    {
      "year": 2800,
      "event": "Permanent habitats exist in the outer Solar System.",
      "detail": "They rely on fusion power, local ice, and closed-loop ecosystems. The communities are small but stable."
    },
    {
      "year": 3000,
      "event": "A loose Solar System commonwealth coordinates law and defense.",
      "detail": "It is not a single state. It manages migration, property rights, resource claims, and asteroid defense."
    },
    {
      "year": 3300,
      "event": "The first interstellar probe reaches its target star.",
      "detail": "It returns limited data. The mission proves that long-horizon institutions can survive for centuries."
    },
    {
      "year": 3600,
      "event": "Human lineages begin to diverge into posthuman forms.",
      "detail": "Some are genetically altered, some cybernetic, and some digital-first. Debates over personhood and inheritance intensify."
    },
    {
      "year": 4000,
      "event": "Interstellar settlement remains rare but possible.",
      "detail": "Slow generation ships and robotic outposts create a thin frontier beyond the Solar System. Expansion is limited by biology, energy, and time."
    },
    {
      "year": 4500,
      "event": "Earth is treated mainly as a protected cultural and biosphere reserve.",
      "detail": "Heavy industry is mostly off-world. Earth remains symbolic, densely curated, and carefully managed."
    },
    {
      "year": 5200,
      "event": "Asteroid mining supports a large space economy.",
      "detail": "Resource scarcity on Earth becomes less binding. Conflicts recur over orbital slots, resource rights, and taxation."
    },
    {
      "year": 6000,
      "event": "A major near-Earth object threat is deflected without loss of life.",
      "detail": "Planetary defense is routine after centuries of surveys and near misses. The event reinforces cooperation among human societies."
    },
    {
      "year": 7000,
      "event": "Most humans live off Earth.",
      "detail": "The species is distributed across the Solar System and a few nearby outposts. This reduces single-planet extinction risk."
    },
    {
      "year": 8000,
      "event": "Contact with intelligent extraterrestrial life remains absent or inconclusive.",
      "detail": "Life beyond Earth, if found, is microbial or fossilized. Humanity appears to be alone in its local region."
    },
    {
      "year": 9000,
      "event": "Long-lived posthuman cultures dominate.",
      "detail": "Reproduction is often artificial, identity is mutable, and traditional nation-states are historical curiosities. Old human cultures survive as preserved traditions."
    },
    {
      "year": 10000,
      "event": "Humanity persists as a diversified civilization.",
      "detail": "It includes baseline humans, posthumans, and machine-minds. There is no single capital or uniform culture."
    }
  ],
  "humanity_exists_in_10000": "yes",
  "summary": "My best guess is that humanity survives to 10000, but only after passing through serious bottlenecks from climate change, biological risk, AI governance failures, and at least one major war. The human population becomes smaller on Earth, more space-based, and biologically and technologically diverse, so the people of 10000 may not resemble present-day humans. Extinction before 10000 is possible, but I judge continued existence more likely than not."
}
